Z||000018||AusFiero||11-14-2001||08:31 AM||||Found on the web. I am not resposible. <P>BBQ Guinea Pig<P>1/4 cup CRISCO Oil<BR>1 medium onion, peeled and chopped<BR>3/4 cup ketchup<BR>1/3 cup lemon juice or vinegar<BR>3 tablespoons granulated sugar<BR>3 tablespoons prepared mustard<BR>3 tablespoons Worcestershire sauce<BR>1/2 teaspoon salt<BR>1/2 teaspoon freshly ground black pepper<BR>3 pounds bone in Guinea pig pieces <BR>Heat Crisco Oil in small saucepan on medium heat.<BR>Add onion.<BR>Cook 5 minutes, or until onions are soft. <BR>Add ketchup, lemon juice, sugar, mustard, Worcestershire sauce, salt and pepper.<BR>Simmer 20 minutes, stirring occasionally.<BR>Heat grill or broiler while sauce is simmering.<BR>Rinse Guinea pig. <BR>Pat dry. Place on grill on medium heat or<BR>about 8 inches from broiler.<BR>For whole bone-in Guinea pigs:<BR>Baste with sauce after about 20 minutes.<BR>Turn Guinea pig.<BR>Baste again after 15 minutes. <BR>Cook 5 minutes longer or until Guinea pig is no longer pink in center, turning and basting as needed.<P>||||reg||11
Z||000019||8Ball||11-14-2001||09:19 AM||||My wife knows a woman who was working as a missionary in Peru. One time during dinner with a family there she was offered the Whole head of the cooked guinea pig as a sign of respect because she was an honored guest. And she was expected to eat it or be rude to them. This included the brain.<P>The meso-americans used to breed guinea pigs as a food source. And because of the docility they would keep them on shelves in the homestead. They would not jump off. Then when time to cook one, They would just pick a nice one off the shelf and cook him.<P>Randy||||reg||1
